---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../data/site';

const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'Memorials', url: '/memorials/' }];
---

<Page
  title="Memorials & Headstones — East Lothian"
  heading="Memorials"
  description="Headstones, plaques and keepsakes arranged in Dunbar. Cemetery regulations, how long to wait, and what your options are. John Bald Funeral Directors."
  standfirst="There is no deadline on this, and no right answer. Some families decide within months, some take years."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <p>
    A memorial is the one part of a funeral you get to make slowly. Nothing about it is
    urgent, and the ground itself requires you to wait, which turns out to be a kindness —
    most families find they know what they want far better six months on than they did in
    the first fortnight.
  </p>

  <h2>Headstones and grave memorials</h2>

  <h3>How long you have to wait</h3>
  <p>
    Between six and twelve months after a burial, so that the ground can settle. Putting a
    heavy stone on unsettled ground means it will lean, and it will have to come off and
    go back on again. A temporary wooden marker can go up straight away if you would like
    something there in the meantime.
  </p>

  <h3>What the cemetery will allow</h3>
  <p>
    Every burial ground has its own rules — on height, width, material, colour, and
    sometimes on wording. East Lothian Council and Scottish Borders Council each publish
    their own regulations, churchyards often have stricter ones, and natural burial
    grounds usually allow no conventional headstone at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    We will check what is permitted where your family's lair is before you commission
    anything, so nobody has the experience of paying for a stone that cannot be erected.
    The rules for each ground are noted in our{' '}
    <a href="/directory/cemeteries/">cemetery directory</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Adding to an existing stone</h3>
  <p>
    Where there is already a family headstone, a new inscription can usually be added. The
    stone is normally taken away, cut and returned, though some inscriptions can be done
    in place. Matching lettering on an old stone is a craft, and a good mason will tell
    you honestly whether it can be matched or whether the new work will always show.
  </p>

  <h2>Where ashes go</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Interred in a family lair, with the headstone inscribed</li>
    <li>In a garden of remembrance at the crematorium, with a plaque or a rose</li>
    <li>Scattered somewhere that meant something</li>
    <li>Kept at home, in an urn or a casket</li>
    <li>Divided, so that more than one person has some</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is no hurry on this either. We will hold ashes here for as long as you need,
    at no charge. Some families collect them the same week; some take a year to feel
    ready, and that is entirely normal.
  </p>

  <h2>Smaller keepsakes</h2>
  <p>
    Not everyone wants a stone. Other things families ask us for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Jewellery containing a small quantity of ashes</li>
    <li>Glass pieces with ashes set into them</li>
    <li>Fingerprint pendants and cufflinks, taken from the person's own print</li>
    <li>Memorial trees and benches, where the council or landowner permits</li>
    <li>Photograph books and framed orders of service</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you would like a fingerprint taken, tell us before the funeral — it cannot be done
    afterwards. It is the one thing on this page that has a deadline, and it is worth
    knowing about even if you decide against it.
  </p>

  <Callout title="No pressure, ever">
    <p>
      Memorials are the part of this business where families most often feel pushed. We
      will show you what is possible, tell you what it costs, and then leave it entirely
      with you. If you decide a memorial is not for you, that is a complete answer and we
      will not raise it again.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What it costs</h2>
  <p>
    Memorials vary enormously — a simple plaque and a large double headstone are not in
    the same world of price. Cemetery authorities also charge a fee for permission to
    erect a memorial, which is theirs rather than ours. We will get you a full written
    quotation before anything is ordered, and you are free to go to any monumental mason
    you like rather than through us.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="accent">
    <p>
      To talk about a memorial, telephone{' '}
      <a href={CONTACT.phoneHref} class="font-semibold">{CONTACT.phone}</a> or{' '}
      <a href="/contact/">send us a message</a>. There is no time limit on asking.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Page>
